from ../meaning/Facts import Facts
from ../meaning/Conditions import Conditions
from ../meaning/Derivation import SpecificOverGeneral
from ./DetectionRules import DetectionRules

export abstract anchor OpenItemKind as open-item-kind:
    description:: string
    forTheImplementer:: string

export abstract anchor OpenItem:
    id:: string
    kind:: OpenItemKind
    rule:: string
    subject:: null:: string
    question:: string
    candidates:: string[]: []
    facts:: string[]: []
    statement:: null:: string

export anchor OpenItems:
    description:
        An open item is something analysis could not settle: a part nobody
        defined, a sentence with two readings, two facts that cannot both be
        true, a statement no rule could read. Each one is a decision the
        specification left for someone else to make.

        Open items are not failures of analysis. They are among its most
        useful results, because every one of them is a place where an
        implementer would otherwise have made the decision alone, silently,
        and possibly differently from what the author meant.

    fourKinds:
        ```text
        gap             something exists, but not enough is said about it
        ambiguity       what is said can be read more than one way
        contradiction   what is said cannot all be true
        unrecognized    what is said matched no rule analysis has
        ```

        They are the anchors in OPEN_ITEM_KINDS below. Each open item is
        found by one detection rule, listed in @{DetectionRules}.

    whatAnOpenItemHolds:
        Every open item states the question it leaves open, in plain words,
        and points at everything involved. Its shape is the OpenItem anchor
        above.

        ```text
        question    what would have to be decided     What does Button.action do?
        subject     what it is about                  Button.action
        facts       facts involved, by id             description/s2/f1
        candidates  for an ambiguity, each reading
        statement   the statement it came from        Button.description/s2
        rule        the detection rule that found it  introduced-part
        ```

    kinds: {OPEN_ITEM_KINDS}

export open-item-kind Gap:
    description:
        The specification shows that something exists, but does not say
        enough about it to build it. Its action is invoked says Button has an
        action, and says when it runs, but never says what it is.

        A gap keeps what is known apart from what is not. The button is
        slightly dimmed tells analysis that a dimming applies to the button,
        which becomes a fact. How much, by what operation, and whether dim is
        defined elsewhere are not known, and each becomes a gap attached to
        that fact.

    forTheImplementer: Do not fill this in. It has not been decided.

export open-item-kind Ambiguity:
    description:
        The information analysis has allows more than one materially
        different reading of what the specification says, and choosing one
        would need an assumption. Materially different means the readings
        would produce different facts. A sentence that could be phrased more
        neatly but has only one reading is not ambiguous.

        An ambiguity lists every reading as a candidate fact, and none of them
        is used. It is not an error in the specification; it is information
        about where the specification could be read differently by different
        people.

    forTheImplementer: Do not pick a reading. The author has not said which is meant.

export open-item-kind Contradiction:
    description:
        Two or more facts cannot all be true at once. Neither is chosen over
        the other. Both facts are kept, marked contested, and neither is used
        as a requirement or as input to derivation until the specification is
        changed.

        Two facts contradict each other when they have the same subject and
        aspect, their conditions overlap, as defined in @{Conditions}, and
        they disagree: they give different values, one is the negation of the
        other, or one gives a value outside a choice the other defines.

        Some apparent disagreements are not contradictions, because a rule
        settles them. A property an anchor overrides replaces its base's,
        which is Piton's own rule. A transformation of a value, such as
        dim(Button.color), builds on that value rather than disagreeing with
        it. And a general fact with a more specific exception is settled by
        @{SpecificOverGeneral}. Anything else that disagrees is a
        contradiction.

        An aspect has one value under any given condition, unless its
        property is a list or the statement gives a list of values, as in its
        colors are blue and white. Facts are described in @{Facts}.

    forTheImplementer: Do not implement either side. The specification must be corrected first.

export open-item-kind Unrecognized:
    description:
        A normative statement that no pattern could read. Whatever was
        understood about it is kept: its subject, if it resolved, and its
        conditions, if they were recognized. So the button looks better when
        disabled is recorded as an unrecognized statement about Button while
        Button.state = disabled, and nothing more.

    forTheImplementer: The requirement in this statement was not understood. Ask before acting on it.

export OPEN_ITEM_KINDS:: extends OpenItemKind[]:
    - {Gap}
    - {Ambiguity}
    - {Contradiction}
    - {Unrecognized}
